WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.260
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 89.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.540
It's June 3, 2026.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.960
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.600
Blue Origin plans to fly its new Glenn rocket again

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.260
before a year's end after a launch pad incident

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.620
damaged the gantry but spared key tanks.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.880
The company's CEO stated that propellant tanks

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.800
at Cape Canaveral pad 36 survived the incident intact

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.320
along with a nearby processing hanger.

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.360
The main support gantry sustained damage

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.140
but can be repaired in place without full reconstruction.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.140
This timeline keeps the vehicle on track

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.400
for its first orbital attempt after earlier ground tests.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.940
Observers will watch for pad work completion

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.700
and any static fire attempts in the coming months.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.720
Now shifting to a rare stellar alignment.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.300
A triple star system stands out

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.720
as one of the best studied stellar trios

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.160
because all three stars eclipse one another

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:56.680
from our line of sight.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.800
Long-term photometry has mapped the precise orbital periods

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.840
that produce these overlapping events.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.400
The geometry lets astronomers measure

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.860
individual stellar radii and temperatures

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.400
with unusual accuracy.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.760
Continued monitoring could reveal

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.700
whether the orbits are stable over longer time scales.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.160
On a different note, two black holes appear together

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.800
for the first time in one frame.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.420
The pair appears in a single frame captured

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.140
by a ground-based observatory

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.360
confirming they share a common galactic environment.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.100
Their proximity allows direct comparison

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.160
of accretion signatures and surrounding gas dynamics.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.740
The detection adds a new data point

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.060
to models of black hole pairing inside merging galaxies.

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.740
Follow-up spectroscopy will test whether the objects

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.800
are actively feeding or quiescent.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.040
Investors are positioning ahead

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.540
of an anticipated SpaceX public offering.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.920
Funds and related equities are seeing increased buying

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.000
as the company prepares its public debut.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.960
Prediction markets have also opened contracts

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.600
tied to valuation milestones.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.980
The move would give broader access

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.800
to SpaceX's starlink and launch businesses.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.480
Analysts expect the offering to set pricing benchmarks

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.160
for other private space firms.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.440
SpaceX is preparing dual starlink launches

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.840
from opposite coasts.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.420
Falcon 9 will launch 24 satellites from Vandenberg

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.840
and 29 from Cape Canaveral within hours of each other.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.960
The Vandenberg flight targets the 200th drone ship landing

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.080
for the booster fleet.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.400
Both missions continue the steady cadence

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.120
of constellation replenishment.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.180
Whether and range availability

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.260
will determine final liftoff times.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.440
The Artemis II crew stopped in Montreal for public outreach.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.000
The four-person crew shared mission updates

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.460
and hardware displays during a stop in the city.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.400
The visit highlights international participation

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.660
in NASA's Lunar Return program.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.740
Canadian contributions to the Orion spacecraft

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:54.480
were featured in the presentations.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.960
The crew's next training milestones

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.660
focus on deep space navigation procedures.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.980
A Paralympian has been selected

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:03.860
as the first disabled astronaut.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.400
The individual will join a future crewed flight

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.500
after completing standard medical and training evaluations.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.700
Selection criteria focused on demonstrated physical capability

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.140
rather than conventional fitness benchmarks.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.200
The decision marks a shift in how space agencies

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.120
assess astronaut eligibility.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:24.580
Mission planners are now reviewing

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.060
hardware accommodations for the upcoming flight.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.240
Saturn's moon Rhea has been confirmed

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.940
in hydrostatic equilibrium.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.900
Cassini close-up images show the icy body

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:37.920
maintains a nearly spherical shape

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.040
consistent with internal fluid pressure.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.980
At roughly 1,528 kilometers across,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.040
Rhea is now the smallest confirmed example

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:48.540
among Saturn satellites.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.380
The finding refines models of how smaller icy moons

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.040
achieve equilibrium shapes.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.720
Feature orbiter data could measure any subtle deviations

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.060
from perfect sphericity.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.640
The surface of series shows unexpected geological complexity.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.540
New mapping presented at the European Geosciences Union

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.780
meeting reveals varied terrain features

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.580
not predicted by earlier models.

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.080
The dwarf planets crust appears to have undergone

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.580
more processing than simple impact gardening would produce.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.680
Spectral data indicate localized compositional differences

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.800
across the surface.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.160
Follow-up studies will examine whether

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:28.320
subsurface processes are still active.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:32.060
You know what's fascinating about neutron star mass limits?

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.440
A neutron star packs the mass of two suns

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.980
into a sphere the size of a mid-sized city.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.780
If you could somehow stand on its surface,

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.500
the gravity would be so intense that a single teaspoon

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.680
of its material would weigh roughly a billion tons on Earth.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:51.640
The object forms when a massive stars core collapses

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.980
after a supernova, squeezing protons and electrons

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:57.320
together until only neutrons remain,

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.420
supported against further collapse

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:01.220
by quantum degeneracy pressure.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.660
Observations of merging pairs and rapidly spinning pulsars

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:07.780
have narrowed the possible mass range,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.040
yet the precise upper limit still floats between roughly

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:12.980
two and three solar masses.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.380
No one yet knows exactly where the boundary lies

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.320
between a stable neutron star and outright collapse

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.500
into a black hole.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.680
New measurements keep refining what we thought we knew

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.140
about the universe's extremes.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:28.480
The small Magellanic Cloud is losing stars

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.420
to its larger neighbor on a galaxy-wide scale.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.560
More than 10 years of vista survey measurements

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.540
track the motions of millions of individual stars,

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.780
revealing systematic title streams

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.800
that were previously only inferred.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.020
The interaction demonstrates how dwarf galaxies

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:48.480
can be reshaped over relatively short cosmic time scales.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.800
Continued wide field imaging will show whether

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.000
the stripping accelerates or stabilizes.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.040
France is preparing two independent launches

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:58.100
in quick succession.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.060
National teams are preparing back-to-back launches

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.860
that will test new small satellite deployment methods

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.860
and a separate scientific payload.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.540
Both efforts build on existing French launch infrastructure

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:10.820
and European partnerships.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:13.860
The schedule calls for sequential liftoffs

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:14.980
within weeks of each other.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.020
The Vera Rubin Observatory has captured a striking view

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:22.300
of a galaxy merger beside undisturbed spirals.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.980
The wide field image shows title streams stretching

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.140
between merging galaxies above two undisturbed

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.320
face-on spirals.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.800
Gravitational interactions are redistributing stars

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.760
across hundreds of millions of years.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.620
The contrast highlights how violent encounters

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.060
reshape galactic structure while leaving neighbors untouched.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:45.280
Repeated imaging will track the mergers progress

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.240
over coming years.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.180
NASA is exploring expanded funding

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.100
for its Mars simulation site on Mount Aloha.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.640
The agency is evaluating whether additional resources

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.180
could expand analog missions at the Hawaiian volcano site.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:03.120
The terrain already hosts tests of rover operations

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.660
and habitat systems.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.800
Expanded funding would support longer duration crew simulations.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:12.500
Project leads are assessing infrastructure upgrades

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.200
needed for sustained activity.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.420
JWST observations of early black holes

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.220
may fall within normal growth ranges after all.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.820
The overmassive objects appeared

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.660
when the universe was roughly two billion years old,

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:29.020
challenging standard accretion timelines.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:32.820
Refined models now suggest some could represent

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.160
the upper tail of expected distributions

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.600
rather than anomalies.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.800
Additional JWST spectra will test whether the sample

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.160
is biased toward the brightest targets.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.440
Keep an eye on the next round of static fire tests

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.740
for new Glenn as pad repairs continue.

